  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.01362v1</id>
    <title>"PRISM" Is Not All You Need: Cross-Topic Name Collisions and Scientific Paper Retrieval</title>
    <updated>2026-11-01T00:00:08Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.01362v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.01362v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Scientific names increasingly connect papers from different research topics, leaving readers to recover a paper's identity from context. We study this convergence in 564,861 AI-related arXiv records. Cross-topic name collision grows 4.33-fold from a pooled 2020-2022 reference to 2026, compared with 1.12-fold within topics. A second sentence encoder reproduces the pattern, and name-group resampling quantifies its uncertainty. Fixed word-frequency bands locate the main increase in an intermediate-frequency vocabulary shared by many research areas. We then measure the ambiguity that remains after supplying research clues. With the same three-term query construction, shared-name targets retain matched identification deficits of 25.7 percentage points under title-based BM25 and 6.9 points under title-plus-abstract E5. Within fixed same-name candidates, listwise language-model ranking reaches 89.2% using the query and candidate titles. Topic-specific comparisons show that context resolves more cross-topic ambiguity. The evaluation separates candidate competition, document fields, and contextual ranking through lexical, dense, and language-model baselines. Source-linked cases and a portable evaluation collection make the phenomenon inspectable and reusable for scientific retrieval and research assistants.</summary>
    <category term="cs.IR" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.DL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-01T00:00:08Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.IR"/>
    <author>
      <name>Jingjie Ning</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Xueqi Li</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Yibo Kong</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.01182v1</id>
    <title>Fast and Exact Relative-Position Attention via Structure-Aware Kernels</title>
    <updated>2026-10-31T18:29:06Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.01182v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.01182v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Relative-position attention mechanisms offer compelling alternatives to RoPE across various transformer architectures, delivering superior performance in domains such as audio. These mechanisms typically modify attention scores by adding content-position or bias-position terms. Unlike RoPE, which encodes position via per-token rotations directly on the queries and keys, relative-position terms generally require pairwise offset-dependent computation or lookups, complicating their integration into fused attention kernels and introducing severe memory, bandwidth, and compute overheads as sequences scale. To address this bottleneck, we propose an exact attention algorithm that incorporates additive relative-position terms into FlashAttention-style tiled computation. Our method (1) uses offset-aware tiling to avoid redundant indexing and memory accesses, (2) computes only the necessary key/query-relation products and reuses them across token pairs in both forward and backward passes, and (3) uses offset-based gradient sharding and aggregation, eliminating atomic contention. We provide a unified, efficient Triton implementation across diverse relative-position attention mechanisms and evaluate it against naive and specialized implementations. For 8K-token sequences, our method speeds up the forward (backward) pass by 1.8-6.4$\times$ (2.0-21$\times$) and reduces peak memory up to 2.9-128$\times$ (1.4-54$\times$) over the strongest baseline for each mechanism, enabling long-context end-to-end transcription that is 1.8$\times$ faster and uses 2.4$\times$ less memory. Our method thus provides a drop-in, hardware-efficient solution for scaling relative-position attention transformers.</summary>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.DC" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-10-31T18:29:06Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>25 pages, 2 figures, 14 tables</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.LG"/>
    <author>
      <name>Delyan Boychev</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Ivo Petrov</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Dimitar I. Dimitrov</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.06844v1</id>
    <title>Where to Connect? Boosting MLLMs via Dynamic Gated Pathways across ALL ViT and LLM Layers</title>
    <updated>2026-11-04T18:01:24Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.06844v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.06844v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>A central yet under-explored question in Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) is where to connect---how visual information from a hierarchical vision encoder should be wired into the layer-wise semantics of a large language model. Mainstream MLLMs inject a fixed single-layer visual representation into the LLM and treat all LLM layers as a single consumer, ignoring that different layers demand different visual granularities. Recent multi-layer fusion methods either compress hierarchical features on the encoder side or rely on predefined sparse or hierarchical layer-to-layer connections, and therefore stop short of modeling adaptive cross-layer interactions between the ViT and LLM hierarchies. We address this gap with DGP (Dynamic Gated Pathways), a routing mechanism that establishes all-to-all connections between every ViT layer and every LLM layer, with each LLM layer dynamically gating its preference over visual layers conditioned on its own semantic state. Built on top of LLaVA-1.5-7B and 13B, DGP delivers consistent gains over the LLaVA-1.5-7B baseline (e.g., +4.2 on SQA$^{I}$, +3.3 on MM-Vet, +3.1 on VizWiz and LLaVA$^{W}$) and surpasses prior multi-layer visual fusion methods on most benchmarks. Layer-wise routing analyses further show that different LLM layers do select different visual granularities on demand, supporting both the effectiveness and interpretability of the proposed dynamic gated pathways. The code is available: https://github.com/YingyingYan1/DGP.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CV" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-04T18:01:24Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>24 pages (10 pages main text + references + appendix), 15 figures. Accepted at the 40th Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2026)</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CV"/>
