  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.07025v1</id>
    <title>LowRAM: Low-Rank Keys and Amortised Values for Composable KV Cache Compression at Test-Time</title>
    <updated>2026-11-04T19:00:06Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.07025v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.07025v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Autoregressive decoding relies on the KV cache, whose memory footprint grows linearly with context length and caps batch size and throughput. Existing post-training methods exploit redundancy across tokens, layers, and feature dimensions, or lower numerical precision. We identify a complementary axis: within each attention head, values are largely predictable from their corresponding keys. We exploit this with value cache amortisation, which fits a lightweight per-head predictor at test time and stores residuals only for the tokens it reconstructs worst, giving $4\times$ near-lossless value compression. The predictor can be initialised by meta-learning for substantially reduced prefill overhead, or fitted from scratch in settings that allow a one-off cost, such as multi-query inference. We pair value amortisation with cross-layer low-rank key compression to produce LowRAM, an asymmetric compression pipeline that stays close to lossless across three model families. By reconstructing only a selected subset of KV pairs at each decoding step, LowRAM can keep decoding overhead minimal and achieve up to $5\times$ higher peak decode throughput at $4\times$ compression. LowRAM further composes with quantisation and eviction, reaching $26.9\times$ compression at $94.5\%$ of baseline performance on long contexts. These results show that treating keys and values as structurally distinct enables efficient, aggressive, and composable KV cache compression.</summary>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-04T19:00:06Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.LG"/>
    <author>
      <name>Teresa Delgado</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Hugo Hazard</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Martin Benfeghoul</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Jun Wang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Zafeirios Fountas</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09634v1</id>
    <title>Steerable Multi-Objective Group-Relative Preference Optimization with Jointly Trained Low-Rank Adapters</title>
    <updated>2026-11-06T15:50:32Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09634v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.09634v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Language model deployment often requires different trade-offs between competing objectives as user preferences change, yet standard post-training methods typically produce a single policy for a fixed objective weighting. We introduce LambdaMix, a new parameter efficient method for learning a preference conditioned family of policies with group-relative online reinforcement learning. LambdaMix jointly trains one low-rank adapter per objective over a frozen base model. One preference vector selects the adapter mixture that generates responses and weights the separately normalized rewards used to update the adapters. A single training run supports changing preferences at inference without further optimization. We experiment with four models on helpfulness and harmlessness and on solving math problems while following instructions. We also evaluate the two largest models on rewriting comments to preserve wording while reducing clues about personal attributes. On the first two tasks, LambdaMix achieves higher hypervolume than all evaluated conditioned and policy merging baselines across all four models. LambdaMix uses fewer training completions and stores fewer adapter parameters than a set of GRPO specialist policies. With architecture and initialization fixed, varying adapter mixtures and objective weights together gives wider score ranges and higher coverage than varying either alone.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-06T15:50:32Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>Preprint. Under review</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CL"/>
    <author>
      <name>Constantinos Karouzos</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Nikolaos Aletras</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.36896v1</id>
    <title>PREEN: Exemplar-Based Color Style Transfer with Smartphone Editor Presets</title>
    <updated>2026-10-27T09:30:35Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.36896v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.36896v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>We present PREEN (Preset Regression via an Exemplar-based Editor Network), which casts color style transfer as regression onto the 29 parameters of a deterministic image editor. Given a content image and a style reference, PREEN predicts a preset, a vector of editor slider values that is interpretable and editor-consumable, not a raster image. The key obstacle is supervision, since an arbitrary real reference lacks a preset ground truth. We therefore supervise with a style-transfer teacher, whose target looks we invert into the editor's parameter space as pseudo-presets, then pretrain a CLIP-conditioned regressor and refine it with LoRA under an image-level CIELAB loss. On the human-curated PST50 benchmark, PREEN applies the reference's color while preserving structural fidelity, leading the baselines on pixel- and perceptual-fidelity metrics. The preset is compact and predicted at sub-second on-device latency. The low-dimensional subspace also exposes a single inference-time strength knob that traces a continuous default-to-vivid trajectory in one forward pass, with every intermediate state a valid editor preset. A two-axis human study shows that raters prefer the vivid end for matching the reference but the default as a standalone photo, a trade-off the knob spans without retraining.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CV" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-10-27T09:30:35Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>Accepted to WACV 2027</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CV"/>
    <author>
      <name>Beomjin Ahn</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.41711v1</id>
    <title>Hierarchical Residuals: A Minimalist Decoupling of Residual Width from Compute Width</title>
    <updated>2026-10-30T13:18:22Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.41711v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.41711v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Residual connections underpin Transformers, but they implicitly tie the residual width carried through depth to the compute width used inside each layer. We introduce Hierarchical Residuals (HiRes), a minimalist architectural change that decouples these dimensions, widening the residual state while keeping per-layer compute width fixed. HiRes groups layers into blocks: standard d-dimensional PreNorm layers operate inside each block, while a wider D-dimensional PostNorm outer residual connects blocks. Learned read/write projections bridge the two spaces at block boundaries, leaving individual Transformer layers unchanged. Across different model sizes and depths, HiRes consistently outperforms vanilla residual addition, with fitted scaling laws indicating a 1.30-fold improvement in FLOPs efficiency. On OLMoE-1B-7B pretraining for 500B tokens, HiRes achieves 0.0208 lower training loss and 1.14-point higher downstream accuracy. HiRes is also competitive with other residual expansion methods such as VWN, mHC and AttnRes with less activation memory and I/O costs. Built from only dense projections and normalizations, it requires neither specialized kernels nor infrastructure-level optimizations. HiRes thus exposes residual width as a practical scaling variable using only standard operators.</summary>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-10-30T13:18:22Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.LG"/>
    <author>
      <name>Ziteng Wang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Jianqiao Lu</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Xinyu Ma</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Jintao Zhang</name>
    </author>
